<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Da Changhe</h1><p class="st-meta">902 CE – 928 CE · capital Yangjumie</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The short-lived dynasty of the Zheng family that replaced Nanzhao in Yunnan in 902 and opened a period of rapid dynastic change around Lake Erhai.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zheng-maisi">Zheng Maisi</a>, a powerful minister descended from a <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/later-tang">Tang</a> official captured by <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/nanzhao">Nanzhao</a>, killed the Meng royal family in 902 and proclaimed the kingdom of Da Changhe, keeping the capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangjumie">Yangjumie</a> near modern Dali.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Little is known of its government, which seems to have continued <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/nanzhao">Nanzhao</a> institutions; sources are sparse. Three <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/zheng-wang-shichong">Zheng</a> rulers reigned, and the court was weakened by rivalry among the great families of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-erhai">Erhai</a> region.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 928 the official <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yang-ganzhen">Yang Ganzhen</a> had <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zheng-longdan">Zheng Longdan</a>, the last ruler, killed and placed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhao-shanzheng">Zhao Shanzheng</a> on the throne as ruler of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/da-tianxing">Da Tianxing</a>. Within a decade the Duan family founded the more durable <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/dali-kingdom">Dali Kingdom</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/da-changhe.jpg" alt="Da Changhe" width="320" height="228"><figcaption>Image: SS · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Later_Liang.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>902 CE – 928 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Yangjumie</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Zheng Maisi, Zheng Renmin, Zheng Longdan</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Bai, Middle Chinese for writing</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Dachanghe</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=915&amp;focus=da-changhe" aria-label="Da Changhe on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7406 1000 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Da Changhe lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7654 1490h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7726 1256h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M7726 1256h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day China. <a href="/atlas?year=915&amp;focus=da-changhe">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
